This product includes software developed independently but interoperates
with, and reimplements algorithms first described and published by, the
following third-party open-source projects. Neither project's source code
is vendored into this repository; both are used as external dependencies
(pip / CMake FetchContent) and as the reference implementations this
project validates against.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Stim
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Copyright the Stim authors.
Homepage:   https://github.com/quantumlib/Stim
License:    Apache License, Version 2.0
            https://github.com/quantumlib/Stim/blob/main/LICENSE

Primary author: Craig Gidney.

Reference:
  Craig Gidney, "Stim: a fast stabilizer circuit simulator",
  Quantum 5, 497 (2021). https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2021-07-06-497

The Pauli frame simulation algorithm (Section 3), the vectorized-frame
gate representation (Section 5.1), and the detector sampling reduction
(Section 5.6) described in the paper above are reimplemented in this
repository's kernel/ and softmodel/ directories as an FPGA-targeted,
from-scratch HLS/C++ implementation. No Stim source code is copied.
Stim itself remains the upstream reference sampler and DEM generator
used throughout this project's validation harness (see tests/).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PyMatching
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Copyright the PyMatching authors.
Homepage:   https://github.com/oscarhiggott/PyMatching
License:    Apache License, Version 2.0
            https://github.com/oscarhiggott/PyMatching/blob/master/LICENSE

Primary authors: Oscar Higgott and Craig Gidney.

Reference:
  Oscar Higgott and Craig Gidney, "Sparse Blossom: correcting a million
  errors per core second with minimum-weight matching",
  arXiv:2303.15933.

PyMatching is used unmodified, via pip, as a CPU decoder and correctness
oracle (see docs/ and Section 8 of the original project brief). No
PyMatching source code is vendored or reimplemented in this repository.

